'''''Hintighernia''''' is a genus of [[Tapejaridae|tapejarid]] [[pterosaur]] from the [[Early Cretaceous]] of the [[Cambridge Greensand]], United Kingdom. The type species is '''''H. edingerae'''''. It was named from a cranium earlier referred to the genus ''[[Ornithostoma]]''.<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Pêgas |first=Rubi V |last2=Bandeira |first2=Kamila L N |last3=Naish |first3=Darren |last4=Holgado |first4=Borja |date=2026-11-01 |title=A tapejarid pterosaur from the Cambridge Greensand (Cretaceous of England), with comments on its neuroanatomy and the biogeography of the group |url=https://doi.org/10.1093/zoolinnean/zlag162 |journal=Zoological Journal of the Linnean Society |volume=208 |issue=3 |pages=zlag162 |doi=10.1093/zoolinnean/zlag162 |issn=0024-4082}}</ref>

[[Harry Govier Seeley]] named some pterosaur fossil fragments present in the collection of the Cambridge [[Sedgwick Museum of Earth Sciences]] as the genus ''Ornithostoma''. Another fragment in the same collection, showing a broken skull crest, was referred to the genus ''[[Ornithocheirus]]''. For many deacades ''Ornithocheirus'' was considered a crested form. However, in 1914 [[Reginald Walter Hooley]] referred the specimen to ''Ornithostoma'', concluding that a crested ''Ornithocheirus'' was "a myth".<ref>Reginald Walter Hooley F.G.S. 1914. "On the Ornithosaurian Genus ''Ornithocheirus'', with a Review of the Specimens from the Cambridge Greensands in the Sedgwick Museum, Cambridge", ''The Anals and Magazine of Natural History'' 78 LXI: 529</ref>

In 2026, after new study of the specimen, it was concluded that it represented a species new to science named ''Hintighernia edingerae'' by Rubi Vargas Pêgas, Kamila Luisa Nogueira Bandeira, [[Darren Naish]] and Borja Holgado. The generic name is a combination of [[Brittonic]] ''hɨnt'', "lord" and ''tɨɣern'', "path", as a translation of the related genus ''[[Tapejara (genus)|Tapejara]]'', the "Lord of the Paths". The [[specific name (zoology)|specific name]] honours the German paleontologist [[Tilly Edinger|Johanna Ottilie Edinger]], the founder of [[paleoneurology]]. In 1927 she had described a natural pterosaur brain endocast found near Cambridge, much resembling that of ''Hintighernia''.<ref>Edinger T. 1927. "Das Gehirn der Pterosaurier". ''Zeitschrift für Anatomie und Entwicklungsgeschichte'' '''83''':105–112</ref>

